    "math_academy_id": "q-106049",
    "knowledge_point_id": "0346056c-cf8e-51bf-92f3-052a0948a6fa",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding the Parametric Equations of a Circle Centered at a General Point",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14011969/assets/2135f70f6703878d5f82d3524c890af08e194d7fdfbe92e97a5305d142f73779.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(2,1)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(4,1)?$",
    "worked_solution": "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(2,1).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
